{
  "comment": [
    "Every resource \"aws_*\" type under infra/modules and infra/roots, and the API actions Terraform",
    "needs to create, read, update, delete and tag it. This file is the reviewable half of",
    "infra/policies/deployment-role-policy.json.tftpl: test/release/deploymentRolePolicy.check.ts walks the",
    "Terraform tree, requires the key set below to be exactly the set of types it finds, and then evaluates",
    "each action against the rendered policy for both roles.",
    "",
    "It exists because the fourth credentialed rehearsal (Actions run 35628963637, 21 September 2026) reported",
    "25 errors in six classes, and every one of them was an action no offline layer in this repository had ever",
    "compared with the Terraform it was meant to apply. The policy was prose in a runbook; this is the list.",
    "",
    "`scope` records how the statement covering the type is narrowed, and the check asserts it:",
    "  arn   - the resource ARN carries the name, so the statement names arn:...:<thing>:<prefix>*",
    "  tag   - the ARN carries no name, so creation is conditioned on aws:RequestTag/NamePrefix and every",
    "          later action on ec2:ResourceTag/NamePrefix or aws:ResourceTag/NamePrefix",
    "  none  - the API has no resource at all; the covering statement's Sid must appear in unconditional_sids",
    "          with the reason, and that list is the whole answer to \"what is unscoped and why\"",
    "",
    "`deny_carve_out` names the Deny statement this type's actions have to survive. A Deny with a Condition or",
    "a NotResource is a scoped deny; a Deny with Resource * and no condition kills the action outright, which",
    "is what NoDeploymentKmsDataAccessOutsideTerraformState did to all eight Secrets Manager entries."
  ],
  "unconditional_sids": {
    "AccountMetadata": "Read-only metadata. Describe, Get and List across the account, which is how Terraform refreshes state and how the release scripts read what they did not create; almost none of these APIs takes a resource, and the ones that do are read-only. No action here changes anything.",
    "OriginAccessControlsCannotCarryATag": "A CloudFront origin access control cannot be tagged and its id is assigned by AWS, so there is neither a name in the ARN nor a request tag to condition on. cloudfront:CreateOriginAccessControl under a RequestTag condition is what denied it on 21 September.",
    "TaskDefinitionApisTakeNoResource": "ecs:DeregisterTaskDefinition, ListTaskDefinitions and ListTaskDefinitionFamilies are documented with no resource-level permission. Registering is separately conditioned on aws:RequestTag/NamePrefix, and a task definition is inert until a service or a run-task names it, both of which are ARN-scoped, and until a task role is passed, which iam:* on role/<prefix>* is scoped by.",
    "AdoptTheVpcDefaultSecurityGroupBeforeItCarriesATag": "The AWS provider revokes the rules on a new VPC's default security group before it tags it, so ec2:ResourceTag/NamePrefix cannot match at the moment of the revoke. The statement is narrowed instead to security groups that carry no NamePrefix tag at all, which excludes every group either environment has ever applied.",
    "UseTheEnvironmentCertificate": "acm:DescribeCertificate on every certificate in the account and region, because the exact ARN is a repository environment secret and shipping it here would put a value in the repository that the workflow deliberately keeps out. Read-only. Pass FSS_POLICY_CERTIFICATE_ARN to narrow it to the one ARN.",
    "CompositeAlarmsAreEvaluatedAgainstEveryAlarm": "CloudWatch authorizes cloudwatch:PutCompositeAlarm against arn:aws:cloudwatch:<region>:<account>:alarm:* rather than against the composite alarm's own name, because the alarm rule references other alarms. David's simulation of 21 September showed exactly that: context.resource alarm:*, implicit deny, no matched statement, with the role's cloudwatch:* scoped to alarm:<prefix>*. A wildcard resource carries no tag either, so no tag condition can narrow this; the statement is the one action on alarm:*, and every other alarm action stays scoped by name."
  },
